window.NPL_SCOUTING_REVIEW = {"revision":"2026-11-24-s2w5-scouting-2","through":"Season 2 · Weeks 1–5","profiles":{"coach_brice":{"points":[{"title":"Scout both Hitmontop and the Scizor opening","text":"Hitmontop led 7 of Brice’s first 9 games, but Scizor + Gyarados opened twice in the three-game Drake set. That pair produced different early threats: Bullet Punch removed Mega Floette on turn 3 of Game 1, while Trailblaze took an incoming Landorus-Therian on turn 1 of Game 3.","prep":"Preserve a Steel-resistant switch and avoid pivoting a chipped Pokémon into Scizor just because Gyarados looks like the obvious threat.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","label":"W1 · Kyle G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","label":"W5 · Drake G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","label":"W5 · Drake G3"}]},{"title":"Keep a single-target line through Wide Guard","text":"Brice attempted Wide Guard 13 times in his first 9 games. Hitmontop covered Garchomp on turn 2 of Kyle Game 3, then Garchomp used its own Protect on turn 3. Hitmontop showed Wide Guard again on turn 2 against Drake in Game 2.","prep":"Have a single-target move that pressures the partner through Wide Guard. Use the Protect turn to threaten the other slot or improve your position.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","label":"W1 · Kyle G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","label":"W5 · Drake G2"}]},{"title":"Watch how early Mega Garchomp Z comes in","text":"After losing the Kyle opener with Garchomp in reserve, Brice brought it in on turn 1 of Game 2 and led it in Game 3. Against Drake it entered from the back in both Scizor + Gyarados games and finished the first with Earth Power into Weezing followed by Draco Meteor into Basculegion.","prep":"Save the Garchomp answer for the switch-in. Against Scizor first, choose an opening that also has a safe follow-up into Garchomp.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2702547589","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2702547589","label":"W1 · Kyle G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","label":"W1 · Kyle G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","label":"W5 · Drake G1"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Several teammates can turn an early trade into a win. Indeedee finished both Kyle wins after Garchomp helped break the opposing four. Against Drake in Game 1, Scizor took Floette, Gyarados took Landorus and Mega Garchomp Z took the final two. That gives Brice a way to lose an opening piece and keep enough damage to close.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2702552043","label":"W1 · Kyle G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","label":"W5 · Drake G1"}]},"weakness":{"text":"In both Wesley losses Garchomp was gone by turn 5, and Indeedee’s three combined KOs could only narrow the margin. Drake showed a second route in Game 3: let Landorus go, take Scizor with Weezing, then use Basculegion’s Aqua Jet to remove a weakened Garchomp. Check which second attacker is in reserve before trading for the first.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714666786","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714666786","label":"W4 · Wesley G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714670281","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714670281","label":"W4 · Wesley G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","label":"W5 · Drake G3"}]},"games":12,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":3},"coach_spencer":{"points":[{"title":"Identify what Grimmsnarl is buying time for","text":"Grimmsnarl led all 13 reviewed games. Its partner changed from Bronzong in two Wesley games to Duraludon for the first two Josh games, then Gengar for the decider. Duraludon used Iron Defense in each Josh game and took Arcanine and Darkrai by turn 3 in the opener.","prep":"Prepare separate answers for Iron Defense and Gengar. Bringing only screen removal can still leave you facing boosted Body Press.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711694913","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711694913","label":"W3 · Wesley G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","label":"W5 · Josh G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","label":"W5 · Josh G3"}]},{"title":"Give Aegislash a reason to hesitate on Wide Guard","text":"Against Drake, Aegislash attempted Wide Guard four times in Game 1 and visibly blocked spread attacks on turns 1 and 2. Drake then took three KOs with Landorus-Therian’s single-target Stomping Tantrum. In Game 2, Spencer used Bronzong’s turn-one Trick Room.","prep":"Bring one reliable targeted attack and be ready to change pace if Bronzong is the partner.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714617893","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714617893","label":"W4 · Drake G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714620637","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714620637","label":"W4 · Drake G2"}]},{"title":"Count Mega Gengar as a reserve win condition","text":"In the Josh decider, Gengar led, withdrew on turn 2 and came back on turn 5. It then took Darkrai, Metagross and Arcanine from turns 7 to 9. Duraludon still contributed the Samurott KO on turn 5.","prep":"Seeing Gengar leave early is a reason to save your answer for later. Avoid spending it entirely on the Steel slot.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","label":"W5 · Josh G3"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Spencer can switch endgames without giving up his familiar Grimmsnarl opening. He won Josh Game 1 with Duraludon and Bronzong Body Press, then won the decider with Mega Gengar taking the last three KOs. Against Wesley he restored his Game 1 Bronzong four after losing with Roserade and won the decider.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","label":"W5 · Josh G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","label":"W5 · Josh G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711703354","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711703354","label":"W3 · Wesley G3"}]},"weakness":{"text":"When you can force a setup piece out and still threaten the replacement, Spencer may have to pay twice for the same position. Arcanine’s turn-one Roar erased Duraludon’s Iron Defense in Josh Game 2. Metagross was then preserved to KO Duraludon and remove screens with Brick Break. Josh also used Roar in Game 3 and lost, so have a plan for Gengar as well.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","label":"W5 · Josh G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","label":"W5 · Josh G3"}]},"games":13,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":3},"coach_austin":{"points":[{"title":"Expect Flutter Mane to get two kinds of cover","text":"Flutter Mane + Magmar led both Week 5 wins over Knox. After Magmar fell on turn 1 of Game 1, Sableye came in and used Fake Out to finish Whimsicott on turn 2. In Game 2 Magmar instead stayed in and used Follow Me on the first two turns while Flutter Mane removed Brute Bonnet.","prep":"Cover the Follow Me opening and the Sableye replacement. A KO on Magmar can change Austin’s support without interrupting Flutter Mane’s offense.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","label":"W5 · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","label":"W5 · Knox G2"}]},{"title":"Treat Sableye as a tempo piece immediately","text":"In its first reviewed set, Sableye used Fake Out and Sunny Day in Game 1. In Game 2 it entered after Ceruledge took Flutter Mane, then used Encore on Ceruledge on turn 6 while Magmar and Meowscarada covered the endgame.","prep":"Keep a line that works after Encore. A support or setup move you can afford to use once may be expensive to repeat with an attacker still available.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","label":"W5 · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","label":"W5 · Knox G2"}]},{"title":"Separate Illumise Tailwind from the fast lead","text":"Illumise led all three Josh games with a different partner each time and set Tailwind on turn 4 in the second. In both Knox wins Austin used Flutter Mane without setting Tailwind and winning Game 1 took just four turns.","prep":"Scout two speed plans. Prepare to pressure Illumise if it appears and to handle Flutter Mane’s immediate pace if Magmar is beside it.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2707973706","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2707973706","label":"W2 · Josh G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2707975943","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2707975943","label":"W2 · Josh G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2707978761","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2707978761","label":"W2 · Josh G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","label":"W5 · Knox G1"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Austin can preserve pressure after one of his main pieces falls. Flutter Mane took three KOs after the turn-one loss of Magmar in the Knox opener. In Game 2 it fell on turn 4, but Magmar’s turn-six Flare Blitz removed Mega Golisopod and Meowscarada finished Ceruledge the next turn.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","label":"W5 · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","label":"W5 · Knox G2"}]},"weakness":{"text":"A late threat that works outside Austin’s preferred move order can still tax his attackers. Ceruledge took Flutter Mane with Shadow Sneak in Knox Game 2. Kingambit’s Sucker Punch finished the last three KOs in Nick’s Game 3 win. Preserve a priority user or a way to punish an attacking turn. Account for possible redirection while Magmar is up.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","label":"W5 · Knox G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710655091","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710655091","label":"W3 · Nick G3"}]},"games":13,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":2},"coach_caleb_b":{"points":[{"title":"Expect a different lead after you show your answer","text":"Caleb B. used three different leads into Kyle’s unchanged Oranguru + Sylveon. Indeedee-F + Raichu lost Game 1. Incineroar + Raichu and then Incineroar + Shuckle won Games 2 and 3. Game 2’s shift changed the primary attack into Sylveon from Raichu pressure to Incineroar’s turn-three Flare Blitz.","prep":"Have a follow-up lead that still functions if Incineroar replaces Indeedee.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","label":"W5 · Kyle G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","label":"W5 · Kyle G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","label":"W5 · Kyle G3"}]},{"title":"Treat Shuckle and Incineroar as a proven pair","text":"Caleb B. led Shuckle in all three Nick games, winning the two with Incineroar beside it. Reunited in the Kyle decider, Incineroar and Shuckle tried Fake Out and Toxic on turn 1, then Shuckle landed Toxic on turns 4 and 7. Poison accounted for two league-credited KOs, and Mega Raichu Y finished the other late targets.","prep":"Bring an early Poison-immune switch or a way to interrupt Toxic. Plan your endgame around both residual damage and Raichu’s coverage.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2707766326","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2707766326","label":"W2 · Nick G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2707777085","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2707777085","label":"W2 · Nick G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","label":"W5 · Kyle G3"}]},{"title":"Indeedee can be an attacker from the back","text":"After leading Indeedee-F in the loss, Caleb B. brought it from reserve in both Kyle wins. Psychic took Pawmot and Venusaur in Game 2 and Pawmot again in Game 3. In Game 2, it took its first KO one turn after Incineroar removed Sylveon.","prep":"Keep a Psychic answer healthy and contest terrain if your plan depends on priority into grounded targets.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","label":"W5 · Kyle G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","label":"W5 · Kyle G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","label":"W5 · Kyle G3"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Caleb B. can win at different speeds against the same opening. In Kyle Game 2, Incineroar and Indeedee split four direct KOs in six turns. The decider lasted 13 turns and two poison faints supplemented Mega Raichu Y’s Focus Blast and Zap Cannon. One defensive plan has to hold up against both.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","label":"W5 · Kyle G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","label":"W5 · Kyle G3"}]},"weakness":{"text":"Getting immediate KOs before his reserve is set can pay off. Maushold took two KOs by turn 2 in Caleb G. Game 1, and Sneasler took Incineroar on turn 1 in Justin Game 2. Both opposing coaches won their sets 2–0. Aim for a clear early target while retaining something that can answer Gliscor or Indeedee after the first KO.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710791945","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710791945","label":"W3 · Caleb G. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714466470","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714466470","label":"W4 · Justin G2"}]},"games":13,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":3},"coach_caleb_g":{"points":[{"title":"Pressure the Maushold + Dracovish lead at the start","text":"They led both wins over Caleb B. and both losses to Justin. In the Caleb B. opener, Maushold took two KOs by turn 2. Justin’s Sinistcha used turn-one Rage Powder in both games, and Maushold was gone by turn 3 in both.","prep":"Plan for immediate Population Bomb and Fishious Rend pressure. Targeting control helped Justin, alongside damage that removed Maushold before it could make a second opening.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710791945","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710791945","label":"W3 · Caleb B. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","label":"W5 · Justin G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","label":"W5 · Justin G2"}]},{"title":"Scout two setters and both directions of speed control","text":"Deoxys-Defense set turn-one Trick Room in both wins over Drake. Smeargle set Trick Room on turn 6 in Caleb B. Game 2 and used Tailwind on turns 3 and 7 in Justin Game 1. Against Austin in Week 4, Smeargle set Tailwind and Trick Room on consecutive turns.","prep":"Keep a way to play under either speed order. Plan how to pressure Smeargle when it comes in, especially if you already committed your own Tailwind.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2706556257","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2706556257","label":"W2 · Drake G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710793706","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710793706","label":"W3 · Caleb B. G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2713328275","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2713328275","label":"W4 · Austin G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","label":"W5 · Justin G1"}]},{"title":"Track whether Dracovish is waiting in reserve","text":"Dracovish took the final three KOs in Caleb B. Game 2 after Smeargle set Trick Room on turn 6. Against Justin in Game 2 it left on turn 1 and returned on turn 6, taking Sinistcha with Crunch on turn 9 before Mega Tyranitar took it out.","prep":"Keep your Water answer if Dracovish withdraws. Make sure it can take a revealed Crunch or has backup once the first speed-control window ends.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710793706","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710793706","label":"W3 · Caleb B. G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","label":"W5 · Justin G2"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Caleb G. has shown that he can change speed plans between games and convert through a different attacker. He lost Drake Game 1 with Heliolisk + Deoxys-Defense, switched to Deoxys-Defense + Smeargle for both wins, and used turn-one Trick Room to give Dracovish a route back into the set.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2706554376","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2706554376","label":"W2 · Drake G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2706556257","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2706556257","label":"W2 · Drake G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2706558822","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2706558822","label":"W2 · Drake G3"}]},"weakness":{"text":"If his opening control gives the opponent time to set up, extra turns can work against him. In Justin Game 1, Corviknight used two Iron Defenses during the Smeargle sequence and took the final three KOs. Caleb G. got one KO across that set. A setup piece that can keep acting through redirection and speed changes can turn those turns into pressure.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","label":"W5 · Justin G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","label":"W5 · Justin G2"}]},"games":13,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":2},"coach_drake":{"points":[{"title":"Keep a Mega Floette answer for the second slot","text":"Mega Floette fell to Scizor Bullet Punch in the Week 5 opener. Drake moved it up beside Weezing in Game 2, where it took Glimmora with Moonblast and Mega Gyarados with Hidden Power. In Game 3 it entered on turn 1 for Landorus and later took Mega Gyarados with Dazzling Gleam.","prep":"Preserve a Fairy answer that can deal with the Pokémon beside Floette. Weezing’s Flamethrower took Scizor in both games where they met.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","label":"W5 · Brice G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","label":"W5 · Brice G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","label":"W5 · Brice G3"}]},{"title":"Expect Landorus to change its attack into Wide Guard","text":"Against Spencer, Wide Guard blocked early spread attacks in Game 1. Drake then used Stomping Tantrum for three Landorus-Therian KOs. It took Hitmontop and Indeedee with the same targeted move in Brice Game 2.","prep":"Give your Wide Guard slot a safe partner or switch against Ground. Blocking spread damage leaves Drake a way to punish the slot you intended to protect.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714617893","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714617893","label":"W4 · Spencer G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","label":"W5 · Brice G2"}]},{"title":"Plan for Basculegion to re-enter after trades","text":"Basculegion took the last two KOs with Last Respects in Spencer Game 2. In Brice Game 3 it led, left on turn 1, returned for the turn-five Aqua Jet KO on Mega Garchomp Z and came back again for Last Respects on turn 7.","prep":"Save your Ghost and Water answers past the midgame. Count what has already fainted and how low your faster attacker can afford to get.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714620637","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714620637","label":"W4 · Spencer G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","label":"W5 · Brice G3"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Drake reversed the Brice set with the same preview six. After losing Game 1, he moved Floette to the lead for Game 2, then started Basculegion + Weezing in Game 3 and used Weezing to remove Scizor before Floette and Basculegion cleaned up. That flexibility makes a successful first-game lead worth reassessing.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718069390","label":"W5 · Brice G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718072051","label":"W5 · Brice G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718075343","label":"W5 · Brice G3"}]},"weakness":{"text":"An opposing speed mode can give Drake’s Ground and Fairy pressure fewer chances to act. Caleb G. set Trick Room on turn 1 in both wins, and Kyle did the same in both wins over Drake while Mega Slowbro took six KOs across the two games. At preview, check how the plan changes with traded-away Aerodactyl and keep a way to exploit your turns of room.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2706556257","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2706556257","label":"W2 · Caleb G. G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2706558822","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2706558822","label":"W2 · Caleb G. G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710527336","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710527336","label":"W3 · Kyle G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710528858","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710528858","label":"W3 · Kyle G3"}]},"games":13,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":3},"coach_josh":{"points":[{"title":"Start with Arcanine and Samurott in your prep","text":"They led all three Spencer games, the first reviewed set after Mega Salamence left the roster. Hisuian Samurott showed Taunt in Games 1 and 3. Arcanine used Roar in Games 2 and 3, including turn 1 of the win to force out an Iron Defense Duraludon.","prep":"Give your first setup Pokémon a way to accomplish something before it can be forced out, and plan for the switch that Roar might leave exposed.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","label":"W5 · Spencer G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","label":"W5 · Spencer G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","label":"W5 · Spencer G3"}]},{"title":"Metagross can remove screens and the attacker behind them","text":"Against Justin, Metagross used Psychic Fangs to take Sneasler in all three games. In Spencer Game 2 it KO’d Duraludon with Stomping Tantrum on turn 8, then removed screens and took Grimmsnarl with Brick Break on turn 9 before finishing Tsareena.","prep":"Save a Metagross answer after your first neutral trade. Resetting screens while it can attack may spend a turn without protecting your win condition.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711190298","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711190298","label":"W3 · Justin G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711196079","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711196079","label":"W3 · Justin G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","label":"W5 · Spencer G2"}]},{"title":"Check whether Clefairy is in the four","text":"Clefairy set Light Screen in all three games against Caleb B. and Reflect in all three against Justin. It did not enter any Spencer game, when Josh used Arcanine, Hisuian Samurott, Metagross and Darkrai throughout.","prep":"Prep for both a protected attacker and the more direct Arcanine + Samurott start. The screen choice has followed the opponent’s damage type when he brings Clefairy.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2703939450","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2703939450","label":"W1 · Caleb B. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711192887","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711192887","label":"W3 · Justin G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","label":"W5 · Spencer G2"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Josh can combine displacement early with a reserve that punishes the rebuild. In Spencer Game 2, Arcanine forced out Duraludon on turn 1. Josh waited until turn 8 to KO it with Metagross and then converted both remaining targets with the same Metagross. It won after that lead had lost Game 1.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718621389","label":"W5 · Spencer G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","label":"W5 · Spencer G2"}]},"weakness":{"text":"Josh kept the same four and lead through the Spencer set. Roar bought time again in Game 3, but Mega Gengar returned from reserve and took Darkrai, Metagross and Arcanine on three consecutive turns. With Salamence off the current roster, watch whether Josh finds a way to handle a fast special attacker while preserving Metagross for the Steel matchup.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718623915","label":"W5 · Spencer G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718627428","label":"W5 · Spencer G3"}]},"games":15,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":3},"coach_justin":{"points":[{"title":"Scout both lead pairs before locking a damage plan","text":"Corviknight + Sneasler led all five Josh and Caleb B. games. Against Caleb B., Tailwind went up on turn 2 in both wins and Excadrill took both last KOs. Against Caleb G. Justin went Tapu Lele + Sinistcha twice and used Rage Powder on turn 1 both times.","prep":"Plan an opening for both Tailwind with immediate pressure and a Psychic Terrain lead with redirection. A single-target answer can lose its intended target into Rage Powder.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711190298","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711190298","label":"W3 · Josh G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714462441","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714462441","label":"W4 · Caleb B. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714466470","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714466470","label":"W4 · Caleb B. G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","label":"W5 · Caleb G. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","label":"W5 · Caleb G. G2"}]},{"title":"Count Corviknight’s boosts and its speed control","text":"In Caleb G. Game 1, Corviknight used Iron Defense on turns 3 and 5, Tailwind on turn 6 and Power Trip for the last three KOs. Smeargle’s own Tailwind and status attempts did not stop it.","prep":"Remove Corviknight’s setup turns or retain a special attacker that can threaten it through Defense boosts. If you share the field with Sinistcha, account for redirection as you choose that attack.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","label":"W5 · Caleb G. G1"}]},{"title":"Expect a different closer behind the same lead","text":"Justin used Tapu Lele + Sinistcha in both Caleb G. games. Game 1 ended with Corviknight, while Game 2 ended with Mega Tyranitar using Knock Off on Deoxys-Defense and Jirachi, then Low Kick on Dracovish. Excadrill closed both wins over Caleb B. the previous week.","prep":"Identify the back slot before spending your Fighting or Ground answer. Stopping Corviknight alone can still leave Mega Tyranitar or Excadrill a clear endgame.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","label":"W5 · Caleb G. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","label":"W5 · Caleb G. G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714466470","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714466470","label":"W4 · Caleb B. G2"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Justin has shown two finishes from one controlled opening. Against Caleb G., Sinistcha used turn-one Rage Powder in both games and kept reappearing while Justin brought his closers in. Corviknight took three KOs in the opener and Mega Tyranitar took three in the second. Justin lost just one Pokémon across both wins.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717669327","label":"W5 · Caleb G. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717671805","label":"W5 · Caleb G. G2"}]},"weakness":{"text":"An early disruption works best when paired with a late answer. Josh took Sneasler with Metagross in all three games, but won only when his remaining Pokémon could outlast Justin’s setup, take away screens and finish Corviknight with Mega Salamence. When Corviknight + Sneasler is the lead, bring a second route to damage Corviknight after your Sneasler answer has acted.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711190298","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711190298","label":"W3 · Josh G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711192887","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711192887","label":"W3 · Josh G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711196079","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711196079","label":"W3 · Josh G3"}]},"games":12,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":2},"coach_knox":{"points":[{"title":"Read Whimsicott’s matchup before choosing your speed move","text":"Whimsicott showed Trick Room on turn 3 of the Caleb G. decider, turn-one Light Screen in the Nick opener and Tailwind on turn 4 of Austin Game 2. In that Austin game it came from the back after Brute Bonnet fell.","prep":"Scout the support move before committing your own speed control, and leave room for Whimsicott to enter after an early trade.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2703691485","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2703691485","label":"W1 · Caleb G. G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714654092","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714654092","label":"W4 · Nick G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","label":"W5 · Austin G2"}]},{"title":"Identify Mega Golisopod’s place in the four","text":"Mega Golisopod closed with the last three direct KOs in both the Caleb G. and Nick deciders. Against Austin in Game 1 it started and KO’d Magmar with Liquidation on turn 1, then fell to Flutter Mane’s Mystical Fire on turn 2.","prep":"Keep an answer that can still hit Golisopod after it has a KO. If it starts, avoid trading away that answer to remove Whimsicott first.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2703691485","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2703691485","label":"W1 · Caleb G. G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714660466","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714660466","label":"W4 · Nick G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","label":"W5 · Austin G1"}]},{"title":"Have a plan for Rage Powder plus Swords Dance","text":"Brute Bonnet and Ceruledge led together in Spencer Game 2 and Austin Game 2. Against Austin, Bonnet used Rage Powder and Ceruledge used Swords Dance on both opening turns. It later took Flutter Mane with Shadow Sneak before Sableye used Encore in the endgame.","prep":"Pressure the setup slot through redirection or save disruption for Ceruledge. Losing a faster attacker to Shadow Sneak can reopen the position even after the redirector falls.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2707936796","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2707936796","label":"W2 · Spencer G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","label":"W5 · Austin G2"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Knox can win a long set by changing how the same Mega Golisopod gets its turns. In Spencer Game 3, Weavile joined it at lead and Golisopod used Throat Chop and Iron Head for KOs. In Nick Game 3, Staraptor was the opening partner and Leech Life repeatedly converted the endgame through turn 16.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2707939352","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2707939352","label":"W2 · Spencer G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714660466","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714660466","label":"W4 · Nick G3"}]},"weakness":{"text":"Austin showed how to keep attacking when Knox changes the support plan. Flutter Mane stayed active after the turn-one Magmar loss in Game 1, then removed Brute Bonnet by turn 2 in Game 2 despite repeated Rage Powder. After Ceruledge took Flutter Mane, the surviving Sableye and Meowscarada handled the endgame. Retain a second attacker for that handoff.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718103580","label":"W5 · Austin G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718105418","label":"W5 · Austin G2"}]},"games":13,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":2},"coach_kyle":{"points":[{"title":"Prepare for both Oranguru and sun at preview","text":"Kyle opened Oranguru + Mega Slowbro three times against Drake and set turn-one Trick Room each time. The next week Charizard + Venusaur led twice against Josh, then Kyle won the decider with Chi-Yu + Sylveon. In Week 5, Oranguru + Sylveon led all three Caleb B. games.","prep":"Bring a four that can handle immediate sun damage and survive the slower Sylveon or Slowbro turns if Oranguru comes instead.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710528858","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710528858","label":"W3 · Drake G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714647705","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714647705","label":"W4 · Josh G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714651213","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714651213","label":"W4 · Josh G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G2"}]},{"title":"Read what Sylveon gets from its partner","text":"Beside Chi-Yu in the Josh decider, Sylveon took three KOs by turn 5. Beside Oranguru against Caleb B., it took Indeedee-F early in Game 1 and later took Incineroar under the slower plan in the decider. Kyle used Trick Room on turn 1 of Games 2 and 3.","prep":"Assign a durable answer to Sylveon at preview. Match your speed plan to the partner you see, and have a move that still works when Trick Room is up.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714651213","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714651213","label":"W4 · Josh G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G3"}]},{"title":"Count Pawmot as a second life for a removed threat","text":"Pawmot used Revival Blessing on turn 5 of Caleb B. Game 1 and on turn 7 of Game 3. In the decider, Oranguru returned on turn 9 after its first poison faint and survived until Mega Raichu Y’s Zap Cannon on turn 13.","prep":"Don’t spend your last Oranguru or Sylveon answer after the first KO if Pawmot is still available. Track what could come back and whether you can safely pressure the revival slot.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G3"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Kyle can change the win condition without losing the ability to finish quickly. Mega Slowbro took six KOs across his two wins over Drake using the same Oranguru opening. Against Josh he moved away from Charizard + Venusaur after losing Game 2 and Sylveon took three KOs in the decider.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710527336","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710527336","label":"W3 · Drake G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710528858","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710528858","label":"W3 · Drake G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714649018","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714649018","label":"W4 · Josh G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714651213","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714651213","label":"W4 · Josh G3"}]},"weakness":{"text":"A familiar opening can give an opponent time to solve the same position. Caleb B. saw Oranguru + Sylveon three times, moved Incineroar to the lead in both wins, and finally used Shuckle’s Toxic to remove both original leads. Pawmot’s revival extended the decider, but Kyle still had to play through the remaining Shuckle and Raichu pressure.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717941165","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717943629","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2717946624","label":"W5 · Caleb B. G3"}]},"games":14,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":3},"coach_nick":{"points":[{"title":"Keeping Farigiraf out early can delay Trick Room","text":"Farigiraf led 9 of 11 games in Weeks 1–4 and both Week 5 wins over Wesley. In both Wesley games, Krookodile forced it out with turn-one Roar. It came back and set Trick Room on turn 4 and turn 6, respectively.","prep":"Plan a second denial turn after that opening Roar or switch. Nick has shown he can bring Farigiraf back safely while the forced-in Pokémon still attacks.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2704389215","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2704389215","label":"W1 · Spencer G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Wesley G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","label":"W5 · Wesley G2"}]},{"title":"Protect two different answers to Nick’s back two","text":"Against Wesley, Ursaluna took four KOs across two games, including a turn-five double Earthquake KO in Game 1 and Ferrothorn with Fire Punch in both. Hydrapple took three in Game 2 with Earth Power and Leaf Storm, including Iron Valiant on turn 2 after the Roar.","prep":"Have a Ground switch or guard for Ursaluna and a separate Hydrapple answer. Don’t give Hydrapple an unchecked hit just to delay Trick Room.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Wesley G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","label":"W5 · Wesley G2"}]},{"title":"Treat Kingambit as another possible closer","text":"Kingambit took the final two KOs in both Spencer games and the last three with Sucker Punch in Austin Game 3. It did not enter either Wesley game. Tinkaton + Farigiraf still opened both games, so the lead alone did not tell you which finisher was coming.","prep":"Keep a late Sucker Punch line if Kingambit is in preview. Avoid spending that slot early until you can account for Ursaluna and Hydrapple.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2704389215","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2704389215","label":"W1 · Spencer G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2704392793","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2704392793","label":"W1 · Spencer G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2710655091","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2710655091","label":"W3 · Austin G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Wesley G1"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Nick turned an interrupted lead into two different winning positions in Week 5. In Game 1, Ursaluna came in on turn 3 and took three KOs over turns 4 and 5. In Game 2, Hydrapple got an early KO, then returned for two late ones. Nick lost just Tinkaton across both games.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Wesley G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","label":"W5 · Wesley G2"}]},"weakness":{"text":"Nick can struggle if the opposing closer profits from Trick Room and stays healthy through the first damage cycle. Knox’s Mega Golisopod converted repeated Leech Life KOs in both wins, including the 16-turn decider. That was before Ursaluna joined the roster, so test the line against the current four and keep an answer to Fire Punch.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714654092","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714654092","label":"W4 · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714660466","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714660466","label":"W4 · Knox G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Wesley G1"}]},"games":13,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":2},"coach_wesley":{"points":[{"title":"Bring separate plans for Primarina and Iron Valiant","text":"Krookodile led beside Primarina in both wins over Brice and beside Iron Valiant in both losses to Nick. Primarina took the closing KO in both Brice games. Against Nick, Krookodile used turn-one Roar twice, looking to displace Farigiraf while Valiant occupied the other slot.","prep":"Prepare a Fairy and Water answer for Primarina and a separate plan for dealing with forced switches early. Keep track of what Roar can bring in.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714666786","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714666786","label":"W4 · Brice G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714670281","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714670281","label":"W4 · Brice G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Nick G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","label":"W5 · Nick G2"}]},{"title":"Don’t rely on your first lead working after Game 1","text":"After losing to Justin with Ferrothorn + Talonflame, Wesley led Iron Valiant + Mienshao in both wins and used turn-one Reflect in both. Mienshao took Excadrill in the decider, and Talonflame still came from reserve for the last two KOs.","prep":"Prepare to attack through Reflect or use special pressure if you showed a physical opening. Keep a Talonflame answer even if it stops appearing at lead.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2703655564","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2703655564","label":"W1 · Justin G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2703658037","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2703658037","label":"W1 · Justin G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2703660871","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2703660871","label":"W1 · Justin G3"}]},{"title":"Check how Ferrothorn will be positioned","text":"Ferrothorn took three KOs after leading with Mega Manectric in the Spencer Game 2 win. Against Nick it came from the back in both games. It used Iron Defense in Game 1 before Ursaluna removed it with Fire Punch, which did so again in Game 2.","prep":"If you bring Fire coverage for Ferrothorn, save it for when Ferrothorn actually enters. Check how Mega Manectric or Krookodile can affect which attacker you want in position.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2711700339","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2711700339","label":"W3 · Spencer G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Nick G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","label":"W5 · Nick G2"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Wesley can turn an early targeted KO into enough space for a second attacker. Primarina dropped Mega Garchomp Z by turn 2 in both Brice games. In Game 1 Mega Manectric took two KOs after that, and Primarina returned to finish Indeedee. That gives the pairing pressure at both ends of the game.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714666786","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714666786","label":"W4 · Brice G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2714670281","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2714670281","label":"W4 · Brice G2"}]},"weakness":{"text":"Turn-one disruption can leave the opponent free to choose a stronger follow-up later. Against Nick, both Roars brought in Hydrapple, and it took Iron Valiant on turn 2 of Game 2. Farigiraf still got Trick Room and Ursaluna’s Fire Punch took Ferrothorn in both games. Look for an opening that remains threatening after the forced switch.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718109366","label":"W5 · Nick G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2718111192","label":"W5 · Nick G2"}]},"games":12,"sets":5,"weekFiveGames":2},"coach_schmitty":{"points":[{"title":"Scout Dragonite’s partner to read the speed plan","text":"Dragonite entered all 13 linked regular-season games. Beside Mega Scizor, it used turn-one Tailwind in both Week 5 wins over Mason. Beside Blissey against Caleb G., the opening instead used turn-one Reflect in Games 1 and 3.","prep":"Have an opening that can withstand the immediate Bullet Punch threat and a way to punish the turn Blissey spends on a screen.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2640878718","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2640878718","label":"S1 W5 · Mason G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2640880863","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2640880863","label":"S1 W5 · Mason G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2626706700","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2626706700","label":"S1 W2 · Caleb G. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2626713799","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2626713799","label":"S1 W2 · Caleb G. G3"}]},{"title":"Use the switches to track where Heatran will finish","text":"Heatran finished both Mason games with Earth Power on Empoleon after Dragonite and Mega Scizor forced early trades. In the Wesley playoff opener Heatran instead took Latias and Victini on turns 8 and 9, following two Primarina KOs.","prep":"Preserve your Heatran answer after an early trade. If Primarina forces it to take a hit first, have another way to pressure Heatran.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2640878718","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2640878718","label":"S1 W5 · Mason G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2640880863","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2640880863","label":"S1 W5 · Mason G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648859058","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648859058","label":"S1 playoffs · Wesley G1"}]},{"title":"Expect his priority user to reposition after a loss","text":"In the Wesley playoff decider, Schmitty switched the lead from Dragonite + Heatran to Primarina + Mega Scizor. Scizor used Protect on turn 1 under Wesley’s Tailwind. Dragonite joined later, set his own Tailwind on turn 3 and Scizor took three different targets from turns 4 to 6.","prep":"Scout both front and reserve speed control. Keep something that can stay in against Scizor’s Bullet Punch and Knock Off when Dragonite enters.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648863185","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648863185","label":"S1 playoffs · Wesley G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648866260","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648866260","label":"S1 playoffs · Wesley G3"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Schmitty could win with several damage types from one position. The Wesley playoff opener went through Primarina and Heatran, while the decider went through Scizor and Lilligant. Against Drake in Week 4, Heatran and Primarina removed two threats early and Dragonite finished with Extreme Speed and Thunder Punch.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648859058","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648859058","label":"S1 playoffs · Wesley G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648866260","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648866260","label":"S1 playoffs · Wesley G3"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2636750886","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2636750886","label":"S1 W4 · Drake G2"}]},"weakness":{"text":"An opponent who survives the first Tailwind turn can change the speed order and put Dragonite on the defensive. In the Brice playoff set, Dragonite used turn-one Tailwind in all three games. Brice brought out Gastrodon + Farigiraf in Games 2 and 3, set Trick Room immediately and removed Dragonite early both times.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648845817","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648845817","label":"S1 playoffs · Brice G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648848867","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648848867","label":"S1 playoffs · Brice G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648850867","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648850867","label":"S1 playoffs · Brice G3"}]},"sampleLabel":"Season 1 · 13 regular-season and 8 playoff games"},"coach_mason":{"points":[{"title":"Scout the attacker paired with Mega Gardevoir","text":"Mega Gardevoir entered all 15 archived Season 1 regular-season games. Gardevoir + Gholdengo was the most common opening at five games. In all three Austin games, Mason chose Roserade as the partner. It removed Mega Swampert and then Barraskewda on turn 1 of the first two games.","prep":"Protect a key Water attacker from the Roserade lead. Against Gholdengo, check the Steel and Ghost damage lines before deciding how to face Gardevoir.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2630957088","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2630957088","label":"S1 W3 · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2636790282","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2636790282","label":"S1 W4 · Austin G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2636793329","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2636793329","label":"S1 W4 · Austin G2"}]},{"title":"Respect Empoleon’s coverage from reserve","text":"Empoleon entered 13 of 15 regular-season games. It took two KOs with Ice Beam in the Knox opener and three different targets with Flash Cannon and Ice Beam in the Caleb G. opener. Against Austin it showed Grass Knot to take Mega Swampert on turn 4 of Game 2.","prep":"Keep track of which coverage move you’ve already seen. A Water-resistant switch can still give Empoleon a useful hit.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2630957088","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2630957088","label":"S1 W3 · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2643666269","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2643666269","label":"S1 W6 · Caleb G. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2636793329","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2636793329","label":"S1 W4 · Austin G2"}]},{"title":"Scout a separate Water plan in Monotype","text":"Mason led Pelipper + Swampert in two of seven Monotype games and set Tailwind on turn 2 in both. His win over Caleb G. instead started Gyarados + Primarina. Palafin took the two Steel targets with Close Combat while Gyarados took Mega Scizor and Gholdengo.","prep":"Bring an answer that can handle Palafin and Gyarados after you see the first two Pokémon. Weather denial covers the rain lead in particular.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2675311878","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2675311878","label":"Monotype · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2675317776","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2675317776","label":"Monotype · Caleb G. G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2675321536","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2675321536","label":"Monotype · Caleb G. G2"}]}],"strength":{"text":"Mason can turn a targeted opening attack into a different endgame. In the Wesley opener, Empoleon and Gholdengo removed Weavile and Latias on turn 3, then Garchomp took Victini and Rotom-Wash. Against Austin in Game 1, Roserade got the turn-one KO and reserve Gholdengo closed on Ferrothorn.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648311352","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648311352","label":"S1 W7 · Wesley G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2636790282","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2636790282","label":"S1 W4 · Austin G1"}]},"weakness":{"text":"Mason logged zero tracked speed-control attempts in 15 Season 1 regular-season games. Schmitty used turn-one Tailwind in both Week 5 wins and took Garchomp with Ice Punch on turn 2 both times. Mason did show Tailwind in the playoffs and Monotype. Scout which setter is actually available before betting that speed will stay fixed.","evidence":[{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2640878718","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2640878718","label":"S1 W5 · Schmitty G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2640880863","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2640880863","label":"S1 W5 · Schmitty G2"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2648859729","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2648859729","label":"S1 playoffs · Knox G1"},{"replay":"gen9doublescustomgame-2675317776","url":"https://replay.pokemonshowdown.com/gen9doublescustomgame-2675317776","label":"Monotype · Caleb G. G1"}]},"sampleLabel":"Season 1 · 15 regular-season and 5 playoff games · 7 Monotype games"}}};
